node provides the Node.js source tree, V8 included, as a linkable library.

  headers    <prefix>/include/node/      Node.js embedding headers
             <prefix>/include/node-v8/   V8's public headers
  libraries  <prefix>/lib/               libnode, and the archives it is made of
             <prefix>/debug/lib/         the Debug ones, when the triplet builds both
  config     <prefix>/share/unofficial-node/

Using it, from CMake:

    find_package(unofficial-node CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(<your target> PRIVATE unofficial::node::node)

`unofficial::node::node` carries the include directories, C++20, the platform
libraries (winmm, crypt32, the Windows manifest and the 8 MiB stack reserve) and
every third-party dependency the library was built against - OpenSSL, zlib, libuv,
ICU, sqlite3, abseil, highway, simdutf, simdjson, nghttp2 and the rest. Do not link
any of them yourself: the library already contains one copy of each, and a second
copy in one process is exactly what must not happen.

The config also publishes the three facts a consumer asks about:

    node_MODULE_VERSION     NODE_MODULE_VERSION, which a prebuilt addon has to match
    node_NAPI_VERSION       the N-API a module gets when it does not ask for one
    node_NAPI_VERSION_MAX   the newest N-API this runtime supports - what decides whether a
                            prebuilt addon may be loaded, and what process.versions.napi reports
    node_LIBRARY_LINKAGE    static or dynamic

Two things to know before you build:

  * The CRT and the linkage have to match what was installed. A Debug consumer
    needs a triplet that builds Debug (a dual one, e.g. x64-windows-static, does);
    a static-CRT install needs CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY set to the same CRT on
    your side. Both mismatches are a clear configure-time error, not a wall of
    LNK2038.

  * A shared library has to be findable at run time. With
    VCPKG_LIBRARY_LINKAGE=dynamic the config links the shared library and nothing
    else, so putting it where the loader looks - or next to your executable - is
    yours to do, as with any shared library.

Features, when the port was built with them:

  * `tools` installs the node executable and npm / npx into <prefix>/tools/node,
    exported as node_TOOLS_DIR, node_EXECUTABLE, node_NPM and node_NPX. (Node.js 26
    does not ship corepack, and neither does this port.)
  * `test-suite` installs Node.js's own suite and its driver into
    <prefix>/share/node/test-suite, exported as node_TEST_SUITE_DIR and runnable as
        python <node_TEST_SUITE_DIR>/tools/test.py \
               --shell=<your executable> --test-root=<node_TEST_SUITE_DIR> parallel

Building this port yourself is a different job - it needs Python, cargo, and on
Windows clang-cl - and docs/build.md covers it. What the delivered library does and
does not guarantee is the README's *Known limits*.
